window.MYTHS_EN = [
{
"id":"china","name":"Chinese mythology","en":"CHINA","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Dragons, celestial courts, and the patient repair of a world",
"description":"Chinese mythology gathers creation accounts, divine genealogies, heroic journeys, and local traditions across many centuries. Its stories intersect with Daoism, Buddhism, ancestral rites, and popular religion without forming one fixed canon. Dragons shape waters, immortals cross mountains, and culture heroes negotiate the fragile relationship between human life and cosmic order.",
"color":"#B84A3D","symbol":"☷","motifs":["Celestial bureaucracy","Dragons and rivers","Immortal mountains"],
"era":"Early accounts survive in texts compiled during the first millennium BCE; later literature, ritual, and oral traditions continually reshape them.",
"quote":"Even a broken sky can shelter the hands that mend it.",
"gods":[{"name":"Nüwa","title":"Restorer of the Sky","desc":"Nüwa repairs the damaged heavens with colored stones and, in influential traditions, shapes human beings, embodying creative care within a world vulnerable to catastrophic disorder."},{"name":"Jade Emperor","title":"Sovereign of Heaven","desc":"The Jade Emperor presides over a celestial administration in many popular religious traditions, receiving petitions while divine officials oversee earthly communities, conduct, and seasonal affairs."},{"name":"Guanyin","title":"Bodhisattva of Compassion","desc":"Guanyin is a Buddhist bodhisattva revered throughout Chinese religious life, responding to suffering through compassionate aid rather than fitting neatly into a single mythological pantheon."}],
"stories":[{"id":"china-1","title":"Nüwa Repairs the Sky","tag":"Creation & Renewal","summary":"When catastrophe damages heaven and floods overwhelm the land, Nüwa gathers colored stones and restores the sky. Her labor makes survival possible, presenting creation not simply as a beginning but as continuing responsibility and repair.","paragraphs":["In an ancient account preserved in the Huainanzi, the supports of heaven break and the earth falls into disorder. Fires rage without dying, waters spread without withdrawing, and dangerous creatures threaten human beings. The story does not begin with a perfect, finished universe. It imagines a world whose very structure can fail, leaving its inhabitants exposed to disaster.","Nüwa answers devastation with practical, extraordinary work. She melts stones of five colors to patch the heavens and cuts the legs from a great turtle to establish new supports. She defeats a black dragon and uses reed ashes to check the waters. Each action addresses a particular danger, bringing the damaged world back within the conditions necessary for life.","The repaired cosmos is not always described as perfectly symmetrical. Related traditions explain the inclination of heaven and the flow of rivers through the world's lasting unevenness. Read together, these accounts make restoration more compelling than simple perfection: the world survives because someone tends its wounds. Details vary across texts and retellings, so no single version exhausts Nüwa's significance."]},{"id":"china-2","title":"Hou Yi and the Ten Suns","tag":"Heroic Deeds","summary":"Ten suns rise together, scorching crops and threatening every living creature. The archer Yi shoots down nine, leaving one to light the world, while later traditions connect his household with Chang'e and the distant moon.","paragraphs":["Some early Chinese traditions imagine ten suns associated with a great eastern tree and a regulated celestial journey. In a famous episode, their orderly succession collapses: all ten appear at once. Fields wither beneath unbearable heat, water becomes scarce, and ordinary life approaches its limit. The crisis turns the life-giving sun into an overwhelming force requiring restraint rather than worshipful acceptance.","Yi, often called Hou Yi in later retellings, takes up his bow. He shoots nine suns from the sky and leaves one to complete the daily passage that humans need. The exploit belongs to a larger cluster of accounts about an exceptional archer confronting threats to the world. Versions differ over his patron, circumstances, and the consequences of his victories.","Later storytelling frequently places Yi beside Chang'e, whose journey to the moon follows her taking an elixir of immortality. The relationship between these episodes developed through centuries of retelling rather than one original, uniform narrative. Together they offer striking contrasts: scorching multiplicity and a solitary moon, public rescue and private separation, heroic power and desires that cannot easily be governed."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Heaven and Earth","desc":"Heaven and earth form a relational cosmic order, interpreted differently across philosophical and religious traditions, with human conduct participating in patterns of transformation and balance."},{"name":"Kunlun","desc":"Kunlun is a mythic mountain associated with divine dwellings and the Queen Mother of the West; its geography changes across literary, religious, and visual traditions."},{"name":"The Underworld Courts","desc":"Later traditions describe judgment after death through courts and officials, drawing upon Buddhist, Daoist, and popular religious ideas rather than one timeless system of belief."}],
"note":"Chinese mythology is not a single creed. Regional worship, textual traditions, and living Buddhist and Daoist communities overlap, while particular figures carry meanings that differ substantially across places and generations."
},
{
"id":"japan","name":"Japanese mythology","en":"JAPAN","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Sacred islands, hidden sunlight, and the presence of the kami",
"description":"Japanese mythology describes islands, lineages, and encounters with kami through early court chronicles and diverse local traditions. Stories of Amaterasu, Susanoo, and the descent of heavenly descendants remain influential within Shinto contexts. Buddhist relationships, regional histories, and continuing ritual practices complicate any simple division between ancient narrative and living religion.",
"color":"#D65A66","symbol":"◉","motifs":["Sacred mirrors","Island creation","Purification and renewal"],"era":"The Kojiki (712 CE) and Nihon Shoki (720 CE) preserve influential court compilations alongside traditions with different local histories.","quote":"A little laughter can open the door that darkness guards.",
"gods":[{"name":"Amaterasu","title":"Kami of the Sun","desc":"Amaterasu is the solar kami central to imperial origin narratives and Ise worship, whose withdrawal and return illuminate the social importance of restored divine presence."},{"name":"Susanoo","title":"Restless Kami of Storms","desc":"Susanoo is a disruptive and heroic kami associated with sea and storms, renowned for challenging heavenly order and defeating the eight-headed serpent Yamata no Orochi."},{"name":"Ōkuninushi","title":"Land-Making Kami","desc":"Ōkuninushi is associated with building the earthly realm, healing, and relationships; narratives of his trials and negotiated authority are especially important in the Izumo tradition."}],
"stories":[{"id":"japan-1","title":"The Sun Behind the Rock Door","tag":"Light & Renewal","summary":"After Susanoo's offenses, Amaterasu withdraws into a cave and darkness spreads. The gathered kami arrange a mirror, ritual offerings, and Ame-no-Uzume's astonishing dance, drawing the sun back into the world through collective action and laughter.","paragraphs":["Susanoo's destructive behavior brings distress into Amaterasu's heavenly domain. In the Kojiki, the violence reaches the weaving hall, and Amaterasu retreats behind the door of a rock cave. Without her presence, the realms lose their light. Countless kami assemble beside a heavenly river to consider how they might persuade the withdrawn sun to return to a world grown troubled.","Their response is carefully staged. They prepare a sacred mirror and other offerings, gather a rooster's call, and set a ritual scene before the cave. Ame-no-Uzume performs a startling dance that makes the assembled kami laugh. Hearing celebration outside when she expects only darkness, Amaterasu becomes curious. The gods tell her that another splendid presence has appeared among them.","Amaterasu looks out and sees her own brightness reflected in the mirror. As she emerges, a powerful kami draws her farther from the entrance, and a sacred boundary prevents renewed retreat. Light returns through cooperation, performance, and ritual ingenuity. The episode's details differ between early chronicles, while its mirror and performance retain importance in later religious and artistic interpretation."]},{"id":"japan-2","title":"Susanoo and the Eight-Headed Serpent","tag":"Monsters & Deliverance","summary":"Exiled Susanoo meets a family awaiting a serpent's final sacrifice. He prepares strong sake, defeats the drunken monster, and discovers a remarkable sword inside its tail, linking a local rescue with the imperial regalia tradition.","paragraphs":["After leaving heaven, Susanoo reaches the land of Izumo and encounters an elderly couple with their daughter Kushinada-hime. Their grief has a terrible explanation: the enormous serpent Yamata no Orochi has already taken seven daughters and will soon return for the eighth. Its many heads and tails stretch across a landscape of mountains and valleys, making fear itself seem geographical.","Susanoo offers help and arranges a stratagem. Strong sake is prepared and set behind a barrier with eight openings, giving each of the serpent's heads a place to drink. When the creature arrives, it consumes the liquor and falls asleep. Susanoo then attacks with his sword, defeating the monster through planning and opportunity rather than a simple contest of strength.","Inside a tail, Susanoo discovers an extraordinary sword, later associated with Kusanagi, one of Japan's imperial regalia. He presents the blade to Amaterasu in the traditional account, connecting his earthly victory with heavenly authority. He then establishes a new life with Kushinada-hime. The tale links rescue, marriage, landscape, and political symbolism, but those connections should not erase its specifically Izumo setting."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Takamagahara","desc":"Takamagahara, the High Plain of Heaven, houses heavenly kami in influential chronicles; its relationship with earthly places expresses sacred genealogy rather than straightforward physical geography."},{"name":"The Central Land","desc":"Ashihara no Nakatsukuni is the earthly realm described between heavenly and otherworldly domains, where land-making, divine negotiations, and human ancestry unfold in early court narratives."},{"name":"Yomi","desc":"Yomi is a shadowed realm associated with death in the Izanagi and Izanami story, distinct from later Buddhist hells and other Japanese visions of afterlife."}],
"note":"Kami should not automatically be equated with Western gods. Shinto practices remain living traditions, and court mythology represents only part of Japan's diverse sacred landscapes, histories, and community ritual life."
},
{
"id":"greek","name":"Greek mythology","en":"GREECE","region":"europe","regionLabel":"Europe","subtitle":"Immortal rivalries and mortal choices beneath the bright Olympian sky",
"description":"Greek mythology explores divine power, heroic ambition, kinship, and the uncertain boundaries of human life. Epic, drama, hymns, art, and local worship preserve overlapping versions rather than a single scripture. Olympian gods share a wider landscape with Titans, nymphs, ancestors, and powers whose stories continually question justice, suffering, and fate.",
"color":"#4F85B0","symbol":"Δ","motifs":["Olympian heights","Heroic journeys","Fate and transformation"],"era":"Mycenaean records attest some divine names; surviving narrative sources include archaic poetry from roughly the eighth century BCE and later traditions.","quote":"The road to glory casts a shadow longer than the spear.",
"gods":[{"name":"Zeus","title":"Keeper of Thunder and Oaths","desc":"Zeus rules the Olympian gods and safeguards hospitality, oaths, and authority, although narratives repeatedly expose tensions between his sovereign responsibilities and his own unruly desires."},{"name":"Athena","title":"Goddess of Wisdom and Craft","desc":"Athena combines strategic warfare, skilled making, and civic protection, guiding heroes while embodying the intelligence and disciplined labor associated particularly with the city of Athens."},{"name":"Poseidon","title":"Lord of Sea and Earthquakes","desc":"Poseidon governs the dangerous sea and shaking earth, with horses also sacred to him; his gifts and anger reveal the instability beneath human maritime ambitions."}],
"stories":[{"id":"greek-1","title":"Persephone and the Returning Spring","tag":"Seasons & Separation","summary":"Hades takes Persephone into the underworld, and Demeter's grief stops the earth's fertility. A negotiated return divides Persephone's time between realms, joining agricultural renewal to a lasting story about separation, power, motherhood, and divine obligation.","paragraphs":["In the Homeric Hymn to Demeter, Persephone gathers flowers when the earth opens and Hades takes her below, with Zeus's consent. Her mother Demeter searches for her and eventually learns what has happened. The narrative gives sustained attention to a mother's grief and anger, while the disappearance unsettles relationships among gods, mortals, and the earth that feeds them all.","Demeter withdraws her gifts, and crops fail to grow. Humanity faces famine, while the gods risk losing the offerings that connect them with mortal communities. Zeus must therefore negotiate. Hermes travels to the underworld to bring Persephone back, but Hades gives her pomegranate seeds to eat. That food establishes a connection with his realm that cannot simply be ignored.","A settlement allows Persephone to spend part of the year below and part with her mother. Demeter restores growth, yet the reunion does not cancel the original violence or the recurring separation. Different sources allocate the seasons differently. The hymn also connects its narrative with Eleusis, where celebrated mysteries offered initiates religious experiences whose full ritual content was not publicly disclosed."]},{"id":"greek-2","title":"Theseus and the Labyrinth","tag":"Heroes & Monsters","summary":"Theseus enters Crete's labyrinth to confront the Minotaur, aided by Ariadne's thread. His escape saves the Athenian youths, but forgotten sails bring tragedy home, making victory inseparable from abandoned promises and the cost of inattention.","paragraphs":["Athens must send young people to Crete, where the Minotaur waits inside a labyrinth designed by Daedalus. The creature, born into a troubled royal household, has a human body and a bull's head. Theseus joins the doomed company, determined to end the tribute. His challenge is double: survive the monster and escape a building constructed to defeat ordinary memory.","Ariadne, daughter of King Minos, helps Theseus with a thread that will mark his passage through the maze. He secures its beginning at the entrance, reaches the Minotaur, and kills it. Following the thread outward, he leads the others toward freedom. The celebrated victory therefore depends not only on heroic strength but also on another person's knowledge, trust, and practical ingenuity.","The homeward voyage darkens the triumph. Ariadne is left on Naxos in accounts that differ sharply over responsibility and divine intervention. Theseus also neglects to replace his ship's dark sails with the promised signal of success. Seeing them, his father Aegeus believes him dead and plunges into the sea. The maze is conquered, but human obligations prove harder to navigate."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Olympus","desc":"Olympus is the gods' elevated dwelling in epic imagination, associated with a real mountain yet described through luminous palaces, feasts, and divine assemblies beyond ordinary access."},{"name":"The Underworld","desc":"The underworld receives the dead beneath Hades and Persephone, containing varied landscapes and punishments that differ considerably between epic, philosophical, mystery, and later literary traditions."},{"name":"Oceanus","desc":"Oceanus is both a primordial divine figure and the encircling river at the world's edges in early poetry, marking distant boundaries of familiar human geography."}],
"note":"Greek myths vary by author, city, period, and ritual setting. Familiar modern versions often combine incompatible ancient accounts, while ancient worship involved much more than stories about Olympian family relationships."
},
{
"id":"norse","name":"Norse mythology","en":"NORSE","region":"europe","regionLabel":"Europe","subtitle":"Oaths, wandering wisdom, and courage beneath the world's branching tree",
"description":"Norse mythology follows gods and other beings through a cosmos shaped by danger, exchange, and approaching catastrophe. Odin seeks knowledge, Thor protects communities, and Loki unsettles fragile alliances. Surviving medieval Icelandic writings preserve essential narratives, but they reflect selective transmission after conversion rather than an unchanged record of pre-Christian belief.",
"color":"#708C9E","symbol":"ᛉ","motifs":["World tree","Binding oaths","Twilight and renewal"],"era":"Most extensive narratives survive in thirteenth-century Icelandic manuscripts, drawing on older poetry and traditions whose dates and regional circulation remain debated.","quote":"A branch may tremble and still hold the weight of dawn.",
"gods":[{"name":"Odin","title":"Seeker of Wisdom","desc":"Odin pursues wisdom through sacrifice, wandering, poetry, and contact with the dead, combining royal authority with troubling strategies that resist any simple model of goodness."},{"name":"Thor","title":"Thunderer and Defender","desc":"Thor wields the hammer Mjölnir against dangerous opponents, protecting divine and human communities while stories also explore his appetite, impatience, courage, and vulnerability to deception."},{"name":"Freyja","title":"Lady of Desire and Seiðr","desc":"Freyja is associated with desire, prosperity, battle dead, and seiðr magic, exercising formidable agency within narratives that frequently make her the object of coercive bargaining."}],
"stories":[{"id":"norse-1","title":"The Theft of Thor's Hammer","tag":"Cunning & Recovery","summary":"When a giant steals Mjölnir and demands Freyja as his bride, Thor must recover his weapon through disguise. A remarkable wedding feast reveals how cunning and cooperation can rescue strength when force alone proves insufficient.","paragraphs":["In the poem Þrymskviða, Thor wakes to discover that Mjölnir has vanished. Loki borrows Freyja's feather garment and travels to the giants' country, where Þrymr admits hiding the hammer deep underground. He will return it only if Freyja becomes his bride. The demand threatens both Freyja's autonomy and the security of gods who depend upon Thor's formidable weapon.","Freyja angrily refuses, and the gods seek another solution. Heimdall proposes dressing Thor as the bride, with Loki accompanying him as an attendant. Thor objects to the humiliation, but the need to recover Mjölnir outweighs his resistance. Veiled and adorned for the ceremony, he enters the giant's hall, where his enormous appetite and fierce eyes nearly expose the deception.","Loki invents explanations for the bride's unsettling behavior, claiming that anticipation has kept her from eating or sleeping. At last Þrymr orders the hammer brought forward to consecrate the marriage. Once it rests within Thor's reach, the disguise has served its purpose. Thor seizes Mjölnir and defeats the giants, ending a poem whose humor plays sharply with gendered expectations and divine dignity."]},{"id":"norse-2","title":"Ragnarök and the Returning Earth","tag":"Endings & Renewal","summary":"Broken bonds and gathering enemies culminate in Ragnarök, where major gods meet their foretold deaths. Yet surviving accounts also imagine a renewed earth, returning figures, and human survivors, setting renewal beside an uncompromising vision of loss.","paragraphs":["Ragnarök is not merely an unexpected battle. Prophecy surrounds the gods with knowledge of approaching destruction: bonds will break, kinship will fail, and a terrible winter will precede the final confrontation. Fenrir the wolf and the world serpent emerge among the threatening powers. The gods prepare despite knowing that preparation cannot guarantee escape from what the poems place before them.","Odin faces Fenrir and is swallowed; Thor kills the serpent but dies after taking only a few steps away from its poison. Other opponents meet in fatal pairings as the world undergoes catastrophic fire and upheaval. The surviving sources do not present every detail identically. Their force lies partly in allowing beloved protectors to confront enemies they cannot defeat without loss.","After catastrophe, some accounts describe the earth rising green from the sea. Surviving gods meet again, Baldr returns, and a human pair continues life beyond destruction. Scholars debate how these passages relate to Christian ideas and older traditions. Their presence does not erase the poem's grief: renewal comes after irreversible deaths, making the future a continuation marked by memory rather than an effortless reset."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Yggdrasil","desc":"Yggdrasil is the great tree connecting cosmic places and beings, sustained and threatened by creatures and forces whose arrangements differ among surviving poetic and prose sources."},{"name":"Asgard and Midgard","desc":"Asgard is associated with the gods and Midgard with human habitation; protective boundaries and dangerous crossings matter more than a consistently mapped set of worlds."},{"name":"Hel","desc":"Hel names both a ruler and a realm connected with the dead, not simply an equivalent of Christian hell or a universal destination for everyone."}],
"note":"Medieval Christian writers preserved much Norse material. Archaeology, poetry, and regional evidence complicate their accounts; modern religious communities also interpret these traditions without representing a single unbroken, uniform historical practice."
},
{
"id":"egypt","name":"Egyptian mythology","en":"EGYPT","region":"africa","regionLabel":"Africa","subtitle":"Solar journeys and renewed life along the river's enduring rhythm",
"description":"Egyptian mythology links divine relationships with kingship, ritual, death, and the maintenance of cosmic balance. Different temples developed distinct creation accounts, while gods could combine forms and identities. Solar rebirth and the restoration of Osiris shaped enduring hopes for renewal, preserved across texts, monuments, images, and changing religious practices throughout millennia.",
"color":"#C59C43","symbol":"☥","motifs":["Solar renewal","Sacred kingship","Balance and afterlife"],"era":"Sources span over three millennia, from early dynastic monuments and Old Kingdom texts to later Egyptian, Greek, and Roman interpretations.","quote":"The river remembers how to begin again without forgetting the dark.",
"gods":[{"name":"Ra","title":"Sun God and Creator","desc":"Ra embodies solar power and creative authority, traveling through sky and underworld while appearing in combinations with other deities across Egypt's changing theological and ritual landscapes."},{"name":"Isis","title":"Protector and Powerful Magician","desc":"Isis restores and protects divine kin through skill, lament, and formidable magic, becoming a major source of maternal protection whose worship spread beyond Egypt itself."},{"name":"Osiris","title":"Lord of the Regenerated Dead","desc":"Osiris is the murdered and restored ruler whose enduring presence among the dead connects legitimate succession, agricultural renewal, and the hope of continued life after death."}],
"stories":[{"id":"egypt-1","title":"Isis Restores Osiris","tag":"Loss & Restoration","summary":"After Seth kills Osiris, Isis searches for her husband and restores his power through mourning and magic. Their son Horus carries the succession forward, while Osiris becomes a model of renewed existence among the dead.","paragraphs":["The story of Osiris survives in Egyptian ritual references, images, hymns, and later extended retellings rather than one authoritative narrative. Its central rupture is the killing of Osiris by his brother Seth. Royal order becomes entangled with family violence, and the dead king's condition threatens more than a household. The possibility of legitimate succession and restored life hangs in the balance.","Isis searches, laments, and works to reassemble or restore her husband, often with help from Nephthys and other divine allies. Her actions combine affection with effective ritual knowledge. Through their renewed union, Horus is conceived and eventually protected through a vulnerable childhood. Different sources arrange these events differently, but Isis consistently acts as a resourceful agent rather than a passive mourner.","Osiris does not simply resume ordinary earthly kingship. He becomes ruler of the dead, while Horus presses his claim within the living divine order. Egyptian funerary practice draws strength from this pattern of restoration, associating the deceased with Osiris. The tale imagines continuity across rupture: death changes existence profoundly, yet care, ritual, and rightful relationship can prevent it from becoming absolute annihilation."]},{"id":"egypt-2","title":"Ra's Journey Through the Night","tag":"Cosmic Renewal","summary":"Each night the sun travels through the Duat, encountering dangers that threaten its return. Divine allies oppose the serpent Apophis, and dawn emerges as a renewed achievement, sustained through cosmic cooperation and Egyptian ritual practice.","paragraphs":["At sunset, Ra's visible passage ends, but the work of the sun continues. Egyptian funerary compositions describe a nocturnal journey through the Duat, a mysterious realm with gates, divine beings, and hidden transformations. These books offer different arrangements of the route. Their shared concern is how the sun can pass through darkness and restore the conditions of life at dawn.","Among the greatest dangers is Apophis, a serpent associated with resistance to cosmic order. Divine companions defend the solar bark, while ritual specialists on earth also participate symbolically in overcoming the threat. Night is therefore neither empty nor inactive. It is a period of necessary struggle and renewal, connecting temple action, royal concerns, and the larger pattern of the universe.","Some compositions emphasize the sun's mysterious union with Osiris, bringing solar movement into relationship with the regenerative power of the dead. Eventually the renewed sun emerges at the eastern horizon. Morning is not treated as a trivial inevitability but as order achieved again. This image should not be mistaken for one fixed map of Egyptian belief, which varied across places and periods."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"The Primeval Waters","desc":"The primeval waters, often called Nun, precede ordered creation in influential accounts, surrounding the emerging world as both a source of possibility and an enduring threat."},{"name":"Sky and Earth","desc":"The sky goddess Nut arches over Geb, the earth god, in familiar images expressing separation, generation, and celestial movement rather than a single scientific geography."},{"name":"The Duat","desc":"The Duat is the hidden realm traversed by the nocturnal sun and associated with the dead, described differently across funerary books, images, and theological settings."}],
"note":"Egyptian religion changed across millennia and communities. Divine combinations were meaningful theological expressions, not mistakes, and later Greek accounts should not replace earlier Egyptian evidence or erase its regional diversity."
},
{
"id":"celtic","name":"Celtic mythologies","en":"CELTIC","region":"europe","regionLabel":"Europe","subtitle":"Otherworld crossings and heroic promises across distinct Celtic speaking traditions",
"description":"Celtic mythologies encompass distinct traditions, especially the surviving medieval narratives of Ireland and Wales. Otherworld encounters, extraordinary craftsmanship, contested sovereignty, and binding obligations recur, but no unified Celtic scripture survives. Christian scribes preserved and reshaped many stories, while archaeology and classical testimony illuminate different, imperfectly connected aspects of earlier religions.",
"color":"#5C9072","symbol":"⌘","motifs":["Otherworld journeys","Binding obligations","Sovereignty and land"],"era":"Important Irish and Welsh narratives survive chiefly in medieval manuscripts; their relationships with earlier oral traditions and continental evidence require careful interpretation.","quote":"Beyond the familiar hill, a promise may become a road.",
"gods":[{"name":"The Dagda","title":"Abundant Father of the Tuatha Dé","desc":"The Dagda is a powerful figure of the Irish Tuatha Dé Danann, associated with abundance, a marvelous cauldron, a potent club, and skilled harp music."},{"name":"Brigid","title":"Irish Figure of Poetry and Skill","desc":"Brigid appears in medieval Irish learning as a daughter of the Dagda associated with poetry; related traditions connect her name with healing, smithcraft, and sanctity."},{"name":"Lugh","title":"Many-Skilled Champion","desc":"Lugh is the versatile Irish champion whose mastery of many arts earns entry to the divine court and whose leadership helps defeat the oppressive Fomorians."}],
"stories":[{"id":"celtic-1","title":"Lugh at the Gate of Tara","tag":"Skill & Sovereignty","summary":"Lugh seeks entry to the court at Tara, where every useful role already has a master. His claim to possess all those skills together changes the question, making versatility the foundation of his emerging leadership.","paragraphs":["In the Irish narrative of the Second Battle of Mag Tuired, Lugh arrives at the royal center of Tara while the Tuatha Dé Danann face difficult relations with the Fomorians. A gatekeeper challenges his right to enter. The court has no place for an unnecessary visitor: admission depends upon offering a skill that the royal household does not already possess.","Lugh names his abilities one after another. He can build, work metal, fight, play music, and practice other demanding arts. Each time, the gatekeeper replies that the household already employs someone qualified. Lugh then changes the terms of the exchange. Instead of asking whether a specialist exists for each craft, he asks whether anyone commands all those abilities together.","The answer opens the gate. Tests of his excellence follow, and Lugh becomes crucial to the struggle against Fomorian domination, eventually confronting Balor in battle. The arrival scene makes an important distinction between possessing a single impressive talent and coordinating many kinds of knowledge. Its setting is specifically Irish, not evidence for one shared narrative belonging to every Celtic-speaking people."]},{"id":"celtic-2","title":"Pwyll and the King of Annwn","tag":"Otherworld & Honor","summary":"Pwyll insults an otherworldly hunt and agrees to exchange appearances with Arawn, ruler of Annwn. A year of restraint and a carefully fought duel transform an offense into lasting friendship between two rulers and realms.","paragraphs":["In the First Branch of the Welsh Mabinogi, Pwyll encounters strange hounds feeding upon a stag during a hunt. He drives them away and sets his own dogs to the carcass. Their master, Arawn of Annwn, confronts him about the discourtesy. Pwyll accepts responsibility and offers compensation, beginning an unusual relationship through acknowledgment of an offense rather than denial.","Arawn proposes an exchange. Pwyll will take his appearance and rule Annwn for a year, then face Arawn's rival Hafgan under a precise condition: he must strike only once. During that year, Pwyll behaves honorably and does not take advantage of Arawn's queen. His success depends on self-command, discretion, and remembering instructions in a world whose rules are unfamiliar.","At the appointed confrontation, Pwyll defeats Hafgan with a single blow and refuses the demand to strike again. The two rulers recover their own forms and discover that each has governed the other's land well. Their friendship endures, and Pwyll gains a title connecting him with Annwn. This Welsh tale values ethical conduct across boundaries rather than treating the otherworld simply as hostile territory."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"The Irish Otherworld","desc":"Irish narratives imagine extraordinary realms reached through mounds, waters, or journeys, where abundance and altered time appear without forming one consistent geography across all sources."},{"name":"Annwn","desc":"Annwn is an otherworld in Welsh tradition, appearing in varied literary contexts; it should not be reduced to either Christian hell or the Irish otherworld."},{"name":"The Inhabited Landscape","desc":"Hills, wells, rivers, and royal centers carry layered meanings in Celtic-speaking regions, but local sacred associations cannot be combined into one universally shared ancient map."}],
"note":"Celtic is a linguistic and historical umbrella, not a single ancient nation or pantheon. Irish and Welsh narratives need distinction, and modern spiritual interpretations require separation from securely documented evidence."
},
{
"id":"roman","name":"Roman mythology","en":"ROME","region":"europe","regionLabel":"Europe","subtitle":"Founding journeys and civic duty beneath the gaze of ancestors",
"description":"Roman mythology connects divine favor, civic institutions, family memory, and the contested beginnings of a city and empire. Roman writers adapted Greek narratives while preserving distinctive rituals and local powers. Stories of Aeneas, Romulus, and the gods frame public identity through obligations whose costs remain visible within the literature itself.",
"color":"#A8544B","symbol":"Ⅴ","motifs":["Founding journeys","Civic obligation","Thresholds and ancestors"],"era":"Literary accounts developed over centuries; major surviving works belong to the late Republic and early Empire and reinterpret traditions of varying antiquity.","quote":"A city begins where memory accepts the burden of tomorrow.",
"gods":[{"name":"Jupiter","title":"Guardian of Sovereignty and Oaths","desc":"Jupiter is a central Roman god of sky, lawful authority, and binding oaths, whose public worship connects the city's security with divine favor and responsibility."},{"name":"Juno","title":"Protector of Community and Women","desc":"Juno holds varied civic and protective roles, especially concerning women and the Roman community, while literary portrayals also explore her resistance to Jupiter's unfolding plans."},{"name":"Mars","title":"God of War and Roman Ancestry","desc":"Mars is a major Roman god associated with warfare and communal protection, remembered as father of Romulus and important within rituals connected with agricultural life."}],
"stories":[{"id":"roman-1","title":"Aeneas Carries the Future from Troy","tag":"Exile & Foundation","summary":"Aeneas escapes burning Troy with his father, son, and household gods, then journeys toward Italy. His mission preserves a people through displacement, but Virgil makes its fulfillment inseparable from grief, difficult choices, and future conflict.","paragraphs":["When Troy falls in Virgil's Aeneid, Aeneas initially imagines dying in its defense. Divine warnings and family responsibility redirect him. He carries his elderly father Anchises, leads his son Ascanius, and takes the household gods into exile. His wife Creusa is lost during the escape. The future begins not with uncomplicated triumph but with a family already marked by separation.","The survivors travel through unfamiliar shores, seeking the homeland promised by prophecy. In Carthage, Aeneas forms a relationship with Queen Dido, herself a founder shaped by displacement. A divine command renews his obligation to continue toward Italy. His departure destroys Dido's hopes and leads to her death, revealing how a celebrated historical mission can inflict suffering upon those outside its promised community.","In Italy, arrival brings warfare rather than immediate peace. Aeneas's descendants will eventually stand within the ancestry claimed for Rome, but Aeneas does not personally found the city. The poem ends with his killing of Turnus, leaving readers with a troubling act rather than a civic inauguration. Duty, divine destiny, grief, and anger remain entangled at the edge of Rome's imagined beginning."]},{"id":"roman-2","title":"Romulus, Remus, and the Founding Boundary","tag":"Origins & Rivalry","summary":"Exposed twins survive through a she-wolf's care and return to challenge an unjust ruler. Their attempt to found a city ends in Remus's death, leaving Rome's origin bound to disputed signs, boundaries, kinship, and violence.","paragraphs":["Roman accounts tell of twins born to Rhea Silvia and fathered by Mars, although details and interpretations vary. A threatened ruler orders the infants abandoned near the Tiber. They survive, famously nursed by a she-wolf, and are raised in a shepherd's household. Their lowly upbringing and remarkable preservation prepare a reversal in which hidden ancestry eventually challenges illegitimate political power.","After learning their origins, Romulus and Remus help restore their grandfather Numitor at Alba Longa. They then seek to establish a settlement of their own. Disagreement arises over leadership and the choice of a site, with the brothers reading bird signs in conflicting ways. The auspices do not dissolve rivalry; instead, debate about what the signs authorize deepens the struggle.","Remus dies during the conflict, in one influential version after crossing the new city's boundary in mockery. Romulus becomes Rome's founder, but fraternal blood marks the achievement. Ancient authors did not all explain the death identically. The story asks what a boundary protects and what founding authority costs, embedding unease within Rome's proudest narrative of origin rather than offering simple celebration."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"The Ordered City","desc":"Rome's sacred spaces, boundaries, and ritual calendar connect civic life with divine relationships, creating a religious geography shaped by institutions, households, neighborhoods, and historical change."},{"name":"The Underworld","desc":"Roman literary underworlds adapt Greek and other materials; Virgil's vision notably links the dead with future Roman history rather than documenting a universally accepted doctrine."},{"name":"Threshold and Household","desc":"Doorways, hearths, ancestral memory, and protective household powers organize everyday sacred relationships, demonstrating that Roman religion extended beyond famous Olympian equivalents and monumental public temples."}],
"note":"Roman religion was not simply Greek religion with renamed gods. Public ritual, household practice, local deities, imperial expansion, and literary adaptation produced changing religious worlds across diverse communities and periods."
},
{
"id":"mesopotamia","name":"Mesopotamian mythologies","en":"MESOPOTAMIA","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Cities, divine kingship, and the fragile gift of mortal life",
"description":"Mesopotamian mythologies emerged among Sumerian, Akkadian, Babylonian, and Assyrian communities whose languages and political worlds changed over millennia. Clay tablets preserve creation poems, divine journeys, flood traditions, and heroic epics. These works explore labor, kingship, mortality, and unstable relationships between cities and gods without forming a single unchanging religious system.",
"color":"#AC875C","symbol":"𒀭","motifs":["Clay and cities","Divine assemblies","Mortality and memory"],"era":"Sumerian and Akkadian compositions survive in tablets from different periods, often through later copies; major traditions span the third to first millennia BCE.","quote":"Clay remembers the hand, though the hand cannot remain.",
"gods":[{"name":"Inanna / Ishtar","title":"Power of Desire and Warfare","desc":"Sumerian Inanna and Akkadian Ishtar are closely associated goddesses of desire, conflict, and authority, whose traditions overlap without making every surviving narrative or cult identical."},{"name":"Enki / Ea","title":"Lord of Wisdom and Fresh Waters","desc":"Known as Enki in Sumerian and Ea in Akkadian traditions, this resourceful deity connects subterranean fresh water, skilled creation, protective counsel, and ingenious divine intervention."},{"name":"Marduk","title":"Babylon's Ascendant Patron","desc":"Marduk rises as Babylon's political and theological prominence grows, becoming the victorious organizer of the cosmos in Enuma Elish rather than every Mesopotamian tradition's creator."}],
"stories":[{"id":"mesopotamia-1","title":"Inanna Descends to the Netherworld","tag":"Descent & Exchange","summary":"Inanna enters her sister Ereshkigal's realm and loses her powers at successive gates. Her rescue requires outside intervention and a substitute, presenting the netherworld as a domain whose claims even a formidable goddess cannot disregard.","paragraphs":["In the Sumerian poem, Inanna turns her attention from the great above to the great below. Before departing, she instructs her minister Ninshubur to seek help if she fails to return. Her destination belongs to her sister Ereshkigal, whose authority cannot be bypassed simply because a visitor is powerful. The journey begins with ambition but also with a carefully prepared contingency.","At seven gates, Inanna is required to surrender ornaments and regalia. Each removal diminishes the signs of her authority until she stands exposed before the netherworld's powers. She is judged, killed, and suspended as a corpse. Ninshubur keeps the promised vigil and appeals for assistance. Eventually Enki fashions emissaries whose attentive responses to Ereshkigal's distress help obtain the means of restoration.","Inanna returns to life, but departure from the netherworld requires a replacement. The ensuing search leads to Dumuzi and a pattern of divided time involving his sister Geshtinanna in related material. This is not merely a story of triumphant escape. Power encounters limits, loyalty matters, and renewed life carries obligations. The Akkadian Descent of Ishtar is a related but distinct composition."]},{"id":"mesopotamia-2","title":"Gilgamesh and the Search for Lasting Life","tag":"Mortality & Wisdom","summary":"After Enkidu dies, Gilgamesh seeks the secret of escaping death. A flood survivor's counsel and a lost rejuvenating plant defeat his hopes, directing attention toward the finite achievements and human responsibilities embodied by Uruk's walls.","paragraphs":["Gilgamesh, king of Uruk, begins the epic as a ruler whose exceptional strength burdens his people. Friendship with Enkidu changes his life, and together they undertake dangerous exploits. When Enkidu dies, grief becomes terror. Gilgamesh recognizes that the same fate awaits him and abandons ordinary royal life to search for someone who has crossed beyond the usual limits of mortality.","His difficult journey leads to Utnapishtim, who survived a great flood and received an exceptional destiny. The survivor's story offers no easy formula for another human being. Gilgamesh also fails a test of wakefulness, discovering how little control he has even over sleep. A chance remains in a plant associated with renewed youth, which he obtains from the watery depths.","Before he can use it, a serpent steals the plant and leaves its old skin behind. Gilgamesh returns to Uruk without the prize he sought. The epic turns its gaze toward the city's enduring walls and the labor and knowledge embodied there. Its manuscripts and versions differ, but the confrontation with grief remains central: wisdom does not abolish mortality, yet it changes how life is valued."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"The Freshwater Apsu","desc":"The apsu is the subterranean freshwater domain associated with Enki or Ea, distinct from the saltwater imagery and divine figure Tiamat in Babylonian creation poetry."},{"name":"The Divine Assembly","desc":"Divine assemblies deliberate authority and destiny in many texts, reflecting varied traditions in which city gods, political change, and cosmic hierarchy continually influence one another."},{"name":"The Netherworld","desc":"The netherworld is commonly imagined as a difficult realm for the dead, governed by its own powers and obligations rather than a reward-based Christian afterlife."}],
"note":"Mesopotamia names a region, not one people. Sumerian, Akkadian, Babylonian, and Assyrian materials differ by language, date, city, and purpose; modern reconstructions must acknowledge gaps and the complexities of translation."
},
{
"id":"india","name":"Indian mythological traditions","en":"INDIA","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Divine presence and moral questions across many living sacred traditions",
"description":"India holds many distinct religious narrative traditions, including Hindu, Buddhist, Jain, and numerous regional and Indigenous inheritances. This selection focuses on widely known Hindu stories, where divine manifestations, devotion, duty, and cosmic renewal intertwine. Sanskrit texts, vernacular literature, performance, and worship preserve multiple interpretations within active, internally diverse religious communities.",
"color":"#D18A42","symbol":"✧","motifs":["Cosmic renewal","Divine manifestations","Devotion and duty"],"era":"Vedic compositions circulated orally during the second millennium BCE; epics, Purāṇas, vernacular works, and living performances developed over many subsequent centuries.","quote":"The ocean yields its wonders only when opposing hands keep working.",
"gods":[{"name":"Vishnu","title":"Preserver and Compassionate Descender","desc":"Vishnu sustains cosmic order and appears through avatāras in many Hindu traditions, receiving devotion through diverse theological understandings that cannot be reduced to mythic symbolism."},{"name":"Shiva","title":"Auspicious Lord of Transformation","desc":"Shiva joins asceticism, household life, dissolution, and grace in varied Hindu traditions, revered as supreme by many devotees and represented through numerous forms and narratives."},{"name":"Devi","title":"The Divine Goddess","desc":"Devi names the Goddess in many Hindu contexts, encompassing forms such as Durga, Lakshmi, and Parvati, with distinct identities and living traditions of profound devotion."}],
"stories":[{"id":"india-1","title":"The Churning of the Ocean","tag":"Cosmic Cooperation","summary":"Devas and asuras churn the ocean to obtain the nectar of immortality. Poison and treasures emerge before the desired prize, revealing a universe where renewal demands cooperation, danger, divine intervention, and difficult disputes over distribution.","paragraphs":["In influential Hindu accounts, the devas seek renewed strength and the nectar of immortality hidden within the cosmic ocean. They enter an uneasy alliance with the asuras, who also desire its benefits. Mount Mandara becomes the churning rod, and the serpent Vasuki serves as the rope. The undertaking requires opposing powers to work together even though their ultimate interests remain sharply divided.","The mountain needs support, and Vishnu takes the form of the tortoise Kurma beneath it. As the ocean is churned, extraordinary beings and treasures emerge, but so does deadly poison. Shiva contains the danger by taking the poison into himself, an episode associated with his blue throat. Creation's gifts arrive together with a threat that could overwhelm those seeking them.","Eventually the nectar appears, often carried by Dhanvantari. Conflict over its possession leads to further divine intervention, including Vishnu's manifestation as Mohini in many versions. Details differ across texts and communities. The narrative is not a straightforward celebration of cooperation: it also asks who receives its rewards, how cosmic order is defended, and what forms of sacrifice make renewed life possible."]},{"id":"india-2","title":"Durga Confronts the Buffalo Demon","tag":"Divine Protection","summary":"When Mahishasura overwhelms the gods, Durga manifests with their combined radiance and weapons. She confronts his changing forms and defeats him, a sacred victory celebrated through diverse living festivals, devotional practices, and regional narrative traditions.","paragraphs":["The Devi Mahatmya describes a crisis in which Mahishasura, the buffalo demon, defeats the gods and seizes their authority. Their anger and energies gather into a brilliant feminine presence. Durga emerges with extraordinary power, receiving weapons and gifts from the divine assembly. The episode presents the Goddess as decisive, not as an incidental helper waiting behind a male champion.","Mounted upon a lion in the familiar account, she confronts Mahishasura's armies and their violent assaults. The enemy shifts forms, resisting the boundaries through which opponents might contain him. Durga responds with unwavering force and control. When he appears as a buffalo and begins another transformation, she pins him down and delivers the decisive blow, breaking the power that has overturned divine order.","The victory remains central to living observances, including forms of Durga Puja and Navaratri, though festival meanings and practices vary by region and community. For devotees, the narrative can express protection, supreme reality, maternal presence, and the defeat of destructive forces. Presenting it as entertainment alone misses its ongoing sacred importance and the theological depth of traditions centered upon the Goddess."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Cycles of Time","desc":"Many Hindu traditions describe vast recurring cycles of creation and dissolution, though their measurements and theological meanings vary across texts, schools, and regional devotional communities."},{"name":"Mount Meru","desc":"Mount Meru forms a cosmic center in several Indian religious cosmologies, including distinct Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain systems that should not be treated as interchangeable."},{"name":"Many Realms","desc":"Heavens, earthly domains, and other realms appear in different Hindu cosmologies, situated within karma and rebirth without constituting one universally fixed map accepted by everyone."}],
"note":"These Hindu selections do not represent all Indian religions or communities. Divine figures remain objects of living devotion; regional interpretations, philosophical schools, and practitioners' own understandings deserve respectful, specific treatment."
},
{
"id":"persia","name":"Persian and Iranian traditions","en":"PERSIA","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Truth, sacred fire, and heroic choices against the growing darkness",
"description":"Iranian traditions include Zoroastrian sacred narratives, royal legends, and the heroic world of Persian epic. They connect truth, moral choice, kingship, and resistance to destructive forces. The Avesta, later Middle Persian writings, and Ferdowsi's Shahnameh preserve different layers, which should not be collapsed into one chronology or a uniform theology.",
"color":"#4D9296","symbol":"✴","motifs":["Truth and choice","Sacred fire","Heroic kingship"],"era":"Avestan traditions have ancient oral roots and complex transmission; Middle Persian works and the Shahnameh, completed around 1010 CE, preserve later layers.","quote":"Truth is a flame that asks the hand to choose.",
"gods":[{"name":"Ahura Mazda","title":"Wise Lord","desc":"Ahura Mazda is central to Zoroastrian worship as the Wise Lord, associated with truth and beneficent creation within theological traditions that have developed over centuries."},{"name":"Anahita","title":"Revered Guardian of Waters","desc":"Anahita is an Iranian divinity associated with powerful, life-giving waters, receiving praise in Avestan tradition and appearing in changing religious and royal contexts over time."},{"name":"Mithra","title":"Guardian of Covenants","desc":"Mithra safeguards covenants and watches against deception in Iranian tradition; related Roman Mithraic practices developed distinct forms and cannot simply be treated as identical worship."}],
"stories":[{"id":"persia-1","title":"Zahhak and the Blacksmith's Banner","tag":"Tyranny & Resistance","summary":"The tyrant Zahhak feeds the serpents on his shoulders with human lives. Kaveh the blacksmith raises his leather apron as a banner, rallying resistance that helps Fereydun overthrow a ruler whose power consumes his people.","paragraphs":["In Ferdowsi's Shahnameh, Zahhak is drawn into destructive choices through the influence of the evil spirit, represented in the epic as Eblis. Serpents grow from his shoulders and demand a terrible diet: human brains. His kingship becomes an image of government sustained by the destruction of its subjects. Families suffer while the machinery of the palace turns killing into routine necessity.","Kaveh, a blacksmith whose sons have been taken, refuses the court's demand that he affirm the tyrant's justice. His protest breaks the appearance of public consent. Raising his leather working apron as a standard, he calls people to resistance and leads them toward Fereydun, the figure destined to challenge Zahhak. An ordinary craft garment becomes a visible alternative to royal terror.","Fereydun defeats Zahhak but, following divine guidance, binds him rather than killing him outright. The tyrant is confined at Mount Damavand in the epic's enduring image. Versions and later interpretations vary, yet Kaveh's banner remains a potent emblem of collective resistance. The story belongs to Persian epic tradition and should not be presented as a direct summary of Zoroastrian ritual doctrine."]},{"id":"persia-2","title":"Rostam and the Son He Does Not Know","tag":"Heroes & Tragedy","summary":"Rostam and Sohrab meet as enemies without recognizing their kinship. When a fatal wound reveals the truth, martial greatness cannot undo the loss, turning a heroic encounter into an enduring meditation on secrecy and war.","paragraphs":["During his travels in the Shahnameh, Rostam meets Tahmineh, and their union produces Sohrab. The child grows into a formidable warrior, carrying knowledge of his father's identity and a token connecting them. Yet politics and warfare place father and son on opposing sides. The audience can perceive a relationship that the combatants themselves fail to recognize in time to prevent disaster.","Sohrab hopes to find Rostam and imagines reshaping the political order with him, but intermediaries and circumstances obstruct recognition. When the two finally face each other in battle, names and signs do not resolve their uncertainty. Rostam, renowned for strength and cunning, struggles against a younger opponent whose power resembles his own. Their heroic qualities sharpen the tragedy instead of providing protection.","Rostam mortally wounds Sohrab and then discovers the identifying token that reveals his son. Recognition arrives when action can no longer be reversed. The epic lingers over grief rather than converting the death into a clean military achievement. Generations of readers and performers have returned to the episode's painful question: what value can heroic reputation retain when it survives by destroying its own future?" ]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Asha and Moral Order","desc":"Asha signifies truth and right order in Zoroastrian tradition, linking ethical life and the created world rather than merely describing an impersonal balance between equals."},{"name":"The Chinvat Bridge","desc":"Zoroastrian accounts describe a bridge of judgment after death, where the consequences of thought, speech, and action matter within a wider vision of ultimate restoration."},{"name":"The Renewed Creation","desc":"Frashokereti names the final renovation in Zoroastrian expectation, anticipating the overcoming of destructive evil rather than an endlessly equal contest without hope of resolution ahead."}],
"note":"Persian epic and Zoroastrian religion overlap historically but are not interchangeable. Zoroastrian communities remain living interpreters of their faith, and Iranian traditions extend beyond modern national, linguistic, and religious boundaries."
},
{
"id":"maya","name":"Maya mythological traditions","en":"MAYA","region":"americas","regionLabel":"Americas","subtitle":"Maize, sacred landscapes, and twin journeys through a challenging underworld",
"description":"Maya traditions belong to many distinct peoples and languages across Mesoamerica, not a vanished civilization with one fixed mythology. Ancient inscriptions and images coexist with colonial manuscripts and living community narratives. This overview highlights K'iche' stories in the Popol Wuj while distinguishing them from broader and specifically Yucatec divine traditions.",
"color":"#4E9C87","symbol":"◈","motifs":["Maize and humanity","Hero Twins","Sacred landscapes"],"era":"Classic-period inscriptions and imagery preserve ancient evidence; the K'iche' Popol Wuj was written alphabetically in the sixteenth century from earlier traditions.","quote":"From a kernel of memory, another season learns its name.",
"gods":[{"name":"Itzamna","title":"Yucatec Creator and Learned Power","desc":"Itzamna is an important deity in Yucatec sources, associated with creation, knowledge, and sacred authority; identifications with specific ancient images require careful scholarly interpretation today."},{"name":"Chaahk","title":"Rain and Lightning Power","desc":"Chaahk is associated with rain, storms, and lightning in ancient Maya evidence, while related living rain traditions take locally specific forms among different Maya communities."},{"name":"Q'uq'umatz","title":"Feathered Serpent of K'iche' Creation","desc":"Q'uq'umatz, the Feathered Serpent, participates in deliberations about creation in the K'iche' Popol Wuj, within a particular tradition rather than one universal Maya divine genealogy."}],
"stories":[{"id":"maya-1","title":"The Hero Twins Outsmart Xibalba","tag":"Trials & Transformation","summary":"Junajpu and Xb'alamq'e confront the lords of Xibalba through endurance, deception, and transformation. Their victories answer an older family defeat, exposing the limits of underworld power in a specifically K'iche' account preserved within the Popol Wuj.","paragraphs":["The Popol Wuj recalls an earlier generation summoned to Xibalba after its ballplaying disturbs the underworld lords. Those players fail the rulers' deceptive tests and are killed. Later the twins Junajpu and Xb'alamq'e inherit the conflict, but they do not repeat every mistake. Their journey is prepared by knowledge, resourcefulness, and a willingness to treat apparently straightforward invitations as dangerous traps.","The twins face testing houses and elaborate attempts to humiliate or destroy them. They use allies, substitutions, and careful observation rather than relying only on strength. Even apparent defeat becomes part of a larger strategy. After death and transformation, they return in altered roles, performing astonishing acts of destruction and restoration that capture the attention of the underworld's powerful rulers.","The lords demand to experience the same marvels and discover too late that the twins will not restore them. Their authority is diminished, and the narrative moves toward the twins' celestial transformation. Names and spellings vary by translation. This account belongs to K'iche' intellectual and sacred heritage; its vivid underworld should not be imposed as the identical cosmology of every Maya people."]},{"id":"maya-2","title":"Humanity Made from Maize","tag":"Creation & Sustenance","summary":"After unsuccessful attempts to create beings capable of meaningful recognition, the makers form humans from maize. Their first extraordinary vision is limited, connecting nourishment, knowledge, gratitude, and human boundaries within the K'iche' narrative of creation itself.","paragraphs":["In the Popol Wuj, the makers seek beings who can speak, remember, and acknowledge those responsible for their existence. Earlier attempts do not fulfill that purpose. Animals cannot provide the desired speech, and other created beings prove unable to sustain the necessary relationship. Creation unfolds through deliberation and revision rather than through a single effortless command that produces a completed human world.","The narrative turns to maize as the substance of human flesh. Animals help reveal the place where nourishing crops can be found, and the preparation of maize makes human formation possible. The first successful people can see and understand with remarkable clarity. Their bodies bind them to the cultivated plant that has supported Maya communities through generations of demanding agricultural knowledge.","Their creators then limit that boundless vision, so human understanding does not equal divine perception. The resulting condition joins dependence with dignity: people can remember, speak, cultivate, and sustain relationships, but they cannot know everything. For living Maya communities, maize carries meanings far beyond a literary metaphor. This account should be approached as one particular heritage within a diverse and continuing cultural world."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Xibalba","desc":"Xibalba is the dangerous underworld of the K'iche' Popol Wuj, governed by formidable lords and trials rather than functioning as every Maya community's identical afterlife."},{"name":"Layered Celestial Realms","desc":"Maya sources describe celestial layers and powerful heavenly beings in varying ways; familiar numbered diagrams often simplify evidence from different regions, periods, and interpretive traditions."},{"name":"The Living Landscape","desc":"Mountains, caves, springs, fields, and communities participate in sacred relationships across Maya traditions, with meanings grounded in specific places, languages, histories, and continuing local responsibilities."}],
"note":"Maya peoples are living, diverse communities. K'iche', Yucatec, and other traditions must not be merged indiscriminately; colonial transcription and translation also shape how surviving narratives reach readers outside their communities."
},
{
"id":"aztec","name":"Mexica and Nahua mythology","en":"AZTEC","region":"americas","regionLabel":"Americas","subtitle":"Successive suns and sacred obligations within a world requiring renewal",
"description":"Often called Aztec mythology, these traditions are more precisely situated among Mexica and other Nahua peoples. Creation, migration, divine sacrifice, and reciprocal obligation shape surviving narratives. Indigenous manuscripts, archaeology, and colonial records provide uneven evidence, while living Nahua communities maintain diverse traditions that cannot simply be equated with imperial religion.",
"color":"#A66D4B","symbol":"⊙","motifs":["Successive suns","Sacred reciprocity","Eagle and cactus"],"era":"Major evidence includes Late Postclassic manuscripts and archaeology alongside sixteenth-century Indigenous and colonial accounts, each shaped by its production and transmission.","quote":"The sun moves because the world is never finished giving.",
"gods":[{"name":"Quetzalcoatl","title":"Feathered Serpent and Culture Bringer","desc":"Quetzalcoatl connects wind, learning, priesthood, and creation in varied Nahua traditions; divine narratives and accounts of a ruler bearing this name require careful distinction always."},{"name":"Huitzilopochtli","title":"Mexica Patron of Warfare","desc":"Huitzilopochtli is the Mexica patron associated with warfare, migration, and solar power, whose importance reflects a particular political and religious history rather than all Mesoamerica."},{"name":"Tlaloc","title":"Power of Rain and Mountains","desc":"Tlaloc governs rain and water-related fertility alongside dangerous storms, linked with mountain places and attendant powers within traditions extending beyond the Mexica capital and empire."}],
"stories":[{"id":"aztec-1","title":"The Making of the Fifth Sun","tag":"Sacrifice & Renewal","summary":"At Teotihuacan, divine volunteers face the fire that will create a new sun. Humble Nanahuatzin leaps first, but further sacrifice is needed before the sun moves, linking cosmic motion with costly action rather than status.","paragraphs":["A Nahua account places the gods at Teotihuacan after earlier worlds have ended. They must bring forth a new sun, and two figures prepare for the task. The richly adorned Tecuciztecatl presents splendid offerings, while the afflicted and modest Nanahuatzin offers what he can. Their contrast makes outward wealth an uncertain guide to the courage that creation will soon require.","When the moment comes to leap into the great fire, Tecuciztecatl hesitates. Nanahuatzin closes his eyes and jumps, and the other follows afterward. Two luminous bodies appear, but their equal brilliance creates another problem. A rabbit is thrown toward the second, dimming it into the moon in the familiar version. Distinction emerges from a sequence of difficult and imperfect actions.","Even then, the sun does not immediately move. Additional divine sacrifice and the work of the wind bring the new order into motion. The account connects the world's continuance with reciprocal giving, but it should not be treated as an uncomplicated endorsement of every imperial practice. Surviving versions differ, and colonial records require attention to both Indigenous voices and the conditions under which they were recorded."]},{"id":"aztec-2","title":"Huitzilopochtli at Serpent Mountain","tag":"Birth & Conflict","summary":"Coatlicue's mysterious pregnancy provokes a violent attack led by Coyolxauhqui. Huitzilopochtli is born armed and defeats the attackers at Coatepec, a foundational Mexica narrative reflected in the ceremonial architecture and monumental imagery of Tenochtitlan's sacred center.","paragraphs":["At Coatepec, or Serpent Mountain, Coatlicue sweeps when a ball of feathers appears. She places it near her body and becomes pregnant. Her daughter Coyolxauhqui and the numerous brothers known as the Centzon Huitznahua regard the pregnancy as a disgrace and plan to kill her. Coatlicue fears their approach, but the child within promises protection against the impending attack.","As the attackers reach the mountain, Huitzilopochtli is born fully armed. With the fiery weapon Xiuhcoatl, he defeats Coyolxauhqui, whose body falls and is dismembered down the slope. He then drives away or kills the brothers. The episode makes birth and battle simultaneous, establishing the patron's power through an extreme rupture within a divine family rather than a peaceful arrival.","The monumental Coyolxauhqui stone at Tenochtitlan's Templo Mayor places this narrative within Mexica ceremonial space. Interpretations often relate the conflict to solar, lunar, and stellar patterns, but not every proposed identification is equally certain. The story also concerns patronage, violence, and imperial authority. Its specificity matters: it is not a generic creation account shared without distinction by all Indigenous peoples of Mexico."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Successive Suns","desc":"Nahua accounts describe earlier worlds or suns destroyed through different catastrophes, situating present humanity within a precarious cosmic history whose details vary among surviving sources."},{"name":"Mictlan","desc":"Mictlan is a realm of the dead reached through a difficult journey in influential accounts; destinations after death often depend on circumstances rather than moral judgment."},{"name":"Mountains and Waters","desc":"Mountains are associated with stored waters and fertility, linking Tlaloc and other powers to places where communities negotiate sustenance, dangerous weather, and reciprocal sacred obligations."}],
"note":"Aztec is a broad modern label. Mexica state religion, wider Nahua traditions, and living Indigenous communities are not interchangeable; accounts of sacrifice require context rather than sensationalism or apologetic erasure."
},
{
"id":"inca","name":"Inca and Andean traditions","en":"INCA","region":"americas","regionLabel":"Americas","subtitle":"Solar ancestry and sacred places across the high Andean landscape",
"description":"Inca narratives connect royal ancestry, sacred landscapes, and the ordered relationships of an expanding Andean state. Colonial accounts preserve differing creation and migration stories, shaped by their authors and informants. Wider Andean traditions exceed imperial religion, while contemporary communities maintain varied relationships with mountains, earth, ancestors, and inherited ritual practices.",
"color":"#BA9545","symbol":"⋈","motifs":["Solar ancestry","Sacred mountains","Reciprocity and place"],"era":"Narratives were recorded chiefly after the sixteenth-century Spanish invasion, drawing on oral histories whose versions reflect regional, political, and colonial circumstances.","quote":"A mountain does not need to speak to hold a promise.",
"gods":[{"name":"Inti","title":"Solar Patron of Inca Rule","desc":"Inti is the sun power central to Inca royal ancestry and state ceremony, although solar devotion existed within a wider Andean landscape of sacred relationships."},{"name":"Viracocha","title":"Creator in Colonial Andean Accounts","desc":"Viracocha appears as a creator and traveling civilizer in several colonial sources, whose differing descriptions require attention to local informants and Christian frameworks of interpretation."},{"name":"Pachamama","title":"Earth and Sustaining Presence","desc":"Pachamama is a living Andean sacred presence associated with earth, place, and sustenance, approached through reciprocal practices that vary among communities rather than one doctrine."}],
"stories":[{"id":"inca-1","title":"Viracocha's Journey of Creation","tag":"Origins & Landscape","summary":"Colonial accounts describe Viracocha ordering the world, bringing forth peoples, and traveling through the Andes. Sacred origins become tied to particular emergence places, while differing versions preserve traces of local histories and the recording process itself.","paragraphs":["Several colonial writers describe Viracocha as a creative power who acts after an earlier, troubled world. Some accounts associate the beginning with the region of Lake Titicaca and the making or ordering of heavenly lights. These sources do not agree in every detail. Their narratives were recorded after conquest, when Indigenous testimony passed through new political pressures and Christian interpretive language.","Viracocha creates or summons peoples and sends them toward the places from which they will emerge into the inhabited landscape. Springs, caves, and other sites become linked with community origins. He then travels, teaching, ordering, and sometimes confronting rejection. The journey makes geography meaningful: particular locations hold memories of how groups came to belong within an interconnected but differentiated Andean world.","Some versions end with Viracocha departing westward across the sea. That image later attracted speculative claims that should not replace evidence from the sources themselves. The lasting significance lies less in identifying a foreign visitor than in understanding creation as the establishment of peoples, places, and relationships. No single colonial version can stand without qualification for all Inca or broader Andean religious thought."]},{"id":"inca-2","title":"The Golden Staff and the Founding of Cusco","tag":"Ancestry & Settlement","summary":"In a famous account, Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo travel from Lake Titicaca carrying a golden staff. Where it sinks into the ground, they establish Cusco, joining solar ancestry, fertile land, and a royal founding mission.","paragraphs":["The account associated with Garcilaso de la Vega tells of the sun sending Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo to guide people toward an ordered way of life. They emerge in the Lake Titicaca region with a golden staff. Their task is to find the place where it sinks easily into the earth, a sign that their journey has reached its intended destination.","Travel carries the pair toward the valley of Cusco, where the staff enters the ground. The sign combines divine authorization with the promise of a suitable homeland. The founders gather people and establish the new center, teaching social and practical skills within the account's framework. Royal origins are thus presented as both a sacred mission and the cultivation of an inhabited landscape.","This is not the only Inca origin narrative. Other colonial sources emphasize the Ayar siblings and emergence from Pacaritambo, offering a different configuration of ancestry, migration, and contested settlement. Garcilaso wrote from his own mixed heritage and historical circumstances. Reading the golden staff story alongside alternatives reveals how origin accounts could express legitimacy without providing a single, universally accepted record of events."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Hanan Pacha","desc":"Hanan pacha commonly refers to an upper world in Andean descriptions, but neat three-level diagrams can oversimplify historically variable understandings of space, time, and relationship."},{"name":"The Inhabited World","desc":"Kay pacha describes this world in many contemporary explanations, where people maintain reciprocal relations with communities, land, ancestors, and sacred presences through locally specific practices."},{"name":"Sacred Places and Wak'as","desc":"Wak'as may be places, objects, or beings with sacred significance, embedded in local histories and relationships rather than a category limited to temples or idols."}],
"note":"Inca state traditions are only part of Andean heritage. Colonial sources need critical reading, and living Quechua, Aymara, and other communities maintain distinct practices beyond simplified diagrams of ancient beliefs."
},
{
"id":"yoruba","name":"Yoruba sacred traditions","en":"YORUBA","region":"africa","regionLabel":"Africa","subtitle":"Orisha, character, and reciprocal relationships within a living sacred world",
"description":"Yoruba sacred traditions center relationships among Olodumare, the orisha, ancestors, and human communities, with strong roots in southwestern Nigeria and neighboring regions. Ifa knowledge, praise poetry, ritual, and oral narratives transmit complex teachings. Diasporic traditions develop related but distinct forms, while practitioners continue interpreting inherited wisdom within changing contemporary circumstances.",
"color":"#B9984E","symbol":"≋","motifs":["Orisha relationships","Ifa wisdom","Character and reciprocity"],"era":"Oral, ritual, artistic, and historical transmission continues today; written collections are comparatively recent and cannot encompass the full scope of community knowledge.","quote":"Wisdom grows where a listening heart makes room for another.",
"gods":[{"name":"Orunmila","title":"Orisha of Wisdom and Ifa","desc":"Orunmila is associated with profound wisdom and Ifa divination, whose interpretive practice involves trained specialists, extensive oral literature, ethical reflection, and ongoing relationships with communities."},{"name":"Oshun","title":"Orisha of River and Flourishing","desc":"Oshun, also written Osun, is associated with rivers, fertility, beauty, and persuasive power, with living worship especially connected to Osogbo and many other devotional communities."},{"name":"Shango","title":"Orisha of Thunder and Authority","desc":"Shango is associated with thunder, lightning, and forceful kingship, connected with Oyo history and honored through varied Yoruba and diasporic practices that retain distinctive forms."}],
"stories":[{"id":"yoruba-1","title":"The First Land at Ile-Ife","tag":"Origins & Sacred Place","summary":"In a widely told creation account, Oduduwa descends to primordial waters with earth and a bird that spreads it into land. Ile-Ife becomes a sacred center, while other Yoruba versions assign different roles and emphases.","paragraphs":["A widely circulated Yoruba account imagines a world initially covered by water. Olodumare authorizes the establishment of habitable land, and Oduduwa descends from above with materials needed for the task. Retellings commonly include a chain, a quantity of earth, and a bird. These familiar elements belong to a living field of narration, not a single text that fixes every detail.","Earth is placed upon the waters, and the bird scratches it outward until a surface expands. Ile-Ife is established as a sacred center, linking creation with a particular place and its continuing significance. The small actions of scattering and spreading matter become world-making labor. A habitable environment appears through prepared materials, divine authorization, and the movement of a creature close to everyday life.","Other accounts give Obatala a leading creative role or describe responsibilities distributed differently among sacred figures. Such differences can carry theological, community, and historical meaning rather than being errors awaiting correction. This brief public retelling does not replace instruction within Yoruba traditions. It introduces the importance of place and relationship while leaving room for the authority of practitioners and locally inherited knowledge."]},{"id":"yoruba-2","title":"Why Oshun Must Be Included","tag":"Wisdom & Participation","summary":"In a widely shared narrative, other orisha try to organize earthly life without Oshun. Their efforts fail until they acknowledge her essential presence, teaching that power cannot flourish when it excludes those whose contributions sustain creation.","paragraphs":["A frequently shared Yoruba sacred narrative tells of orisha undertaking the work of establishing conditions for life on earth. Oshun is present, but the others disregard her participation and proceed as if their own powers are sufficient. What appears to them a practical arrangement becomes a fundamental mistake. Their plans cannot achieve the flourishing they expect from their combined abilities.","When they seek the reason for their failure, they learn that Oshun cannot be treated as unnecessary. In commonly published versions, Olodumare directs attention to the person they have excluded. The remedy requires more than obtaining another tool or performing the same actions with greater force. They must change their relationship with her and recognize the indispensable power she brings.","With Oshun acknowledged, the work can succeed. The narrative has been interpreted through gender, cooperation, fertility, and the limits of arrogant authority, though its meanings exceed a single modern slogan. Public summaries should remain distinct from the fuller knowledge of Ifa practitioners and devotional communities. Its central challenge is clear: collective power is diminished when it refuses to honor an essential participant."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Orun and Aiye","desc":"Orun and aiye name related spiritual and earthly dimensions in Yoruba thought, connected through ritual, destiny, ancestors, and sacred relationships rather than sealed, disconnected universes."},{"name":"Ori and Destiny","desc":"Ori, often translated as head, carries profound significance for personal destiny and spiritual identity, emphasizing individual responsibility within relationships that involve divine and communal support."},{"name":"Ashe","desc":"Ashe is the enabling power through which words, actions, beings, and sacred relationships become effective, understood within living practice rather than as a generic magic."}],
"note":"Yoruba religion is a living, internally diverse tradition, not shorthand for African belief. Diasporic religions share historical relationships but require their own names, histories, and practitioners' perspectives in respectful representation."
},
{
"id":"west-africa","name":"Akan and Ananse traditions","en":"AKAN","region":"africa","regionLabel":"Africa","subtitle":"A spider's clever bargains within a specifically Akan storytelling world",
"description":"This focused selection explores Akan traditions, especially Ananse the spider storyteller, rather than proposing one mythology for all West Africa. Sacred relationships with Nyame and the earth coexist with witty narratives about intelligence, appetite, and social life. Ananse stories also traveled into Caribbean and other diasporic communities through painful histories.",
"color":"#B1764B","symbol":"⌁","motifs":["Spider storytelling","Clever bargains","Wisdom and community"],"era":"Akan oral traditions continue in performance; written collections from different periods capture particular tellings rather than an original, complete, or universally authoritative canon.","quote":"A story escapes every pot that tries to keep it alone.",
"gods":[{"name":"Nyame","title":"Supreme Being in Akan Traditions","desc":"Nyame names the supreme divine being in Akan traditions, associated with the sky and creative authority, though names, theological emphases, and local understandings vary considerably."},{"name":"Asase Yaa","title":"Earth Power and Moral Ground","desc":"Asase Yaa is the sacred earth in many Akan contexts, connected with fertility, livelihood, and moral responsibility, with practices and naming varying among particular communities."},{"name":"Ananse","title":"Spider Trickster and Story Keeper","desc":"Ananse is a clever, fallible spider figure in Akan storytelling, sometimes appearing with human characteristics; his status and significance differ from those of supreme divinity."}],
"stories":[{"id":"west-africa-1","title":"How Ananse Won the Stories","tag":"Cunning & Storytelling","summary":"Ananse asks Nyame to release the stories and accepts an apparently impossible price. By catching dangerous creatures through clever traps, he earns the storytelling treasure, linking narrative ownership with ingenuity rather than physical strength alone itself.","paragraphs":["In a familiar Akan tale, the stories belong to Nyame, and Ananse wants them brought within human reach. The sky being names a formidable price, often requiring the capture of a python, a leopard, hornets, and a fairy. The exact list varies in performance. What matters is the imbalance: a small spider has accepted challenges that would intimidate a far stronger creature.","Ananse meets each challenge by arranging circumstances rather than fighting directly. He persuades the python to lie beside a measuring pole, tricks the hornets into entering a container, and uses a prepared pit against the leopard in common tellings. A sticky figure helps catch the elusive fairy. His successes depend upon understanding curiosity, pride, fear, and the assumptions that make others predictable.","When Ananse presents the required captives, Nyame grants the stories, which become associated with the spider's name. Performances can celebrate ingenuity while also inviting laughter at manipulation and appetite. Ananse is not a model of uncomplicated virtue. As the stories traveled into the diaspora, new communities reshaped them, preserving a capacity to question power through the movements of a small, resourceful figure."]},{"id":"west-africa-2","title":"The Pot That Could Not Hold All Wisdom","tag":"Wisdom & Humility","summary":"Ananse gathers wisdom into a pot and tries to hide it atop a tree. His child's practical suggestion exposes the limits of his supposed monopoly, and the scattered contents make wisdom available beyond one owner alone.","paragraphs":["Ananse decides that he would like to possess all the world's wisdom. He gathers it into a pot and searches for a place where no one else can reach it. A tall tree seems suitable, but climbing while carrying the vessel proves difficult. His plan contains a contradiction: the owner of every answer cannot solve the immediate problem of moving his own burden.","In a familiar version, he ties the pot in front of his body, where it blocks his progress up the trunk. A child watching from below asks why he does not carry it on his back instead. The advice is simple and effective. Ananse suddenly confronts knowledge that has arisen outside the container where he believes all useful understanding has been secured.","Anger, surprise, or embarrassment leads to the pot falling or being broken, depending on the telling. Wisdom scatters into the world and can no longer belong to Ananse alone. The tale resists the fantasy that insight can be collected once and then withheld permanently. Its humor leaves listeners with a social lesson: understanding remains distributed, and even a child may reveal what a clever adult overlooks."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Sky and Divine Authority","desc":"Nyame's association with the sky shapes many Akan narratives, but the relationship between divine transcendence and everyday life cannot be reduced to a single diagram."},{"name":"Earth and Responsibility","desc":"The sacred earth connects livelihood with ethical obligations in Akan communities, where farming, burial, and respect for place carry meanings grounded in particular local practices."},{"name":"Ancestors and Community","desc":"Ancestors remain significant within many Akan religious worlds, linking lineage, moral authority, remembrance, and responsibility without implying that every deceased person automatically holds identical status."}],
"note":"This entry concerns Akan and Ananse traditions, not a unified West African pantheon. Storytelling variants and Caribbean Anansi traditions deserve distinction, while living communities remain authoritative interpreters of their heritage."
},
{
"id":"polynesia","name":"Polynesian mythological traditions","en":"POLYNESIA","region":"oceania","regionLabel":"Oceania","subtitle":"Ocean pathways and ancestral worlds across many distinct island traditions",
"description":"Polynesian peoples maintain related but distinct traditions across an immense ocean, including Māori, Hawaiian, Samoan, Tongan, and Tahitian inheritances. Genealogies link land, sea, ancestors, and divine beings, while voyages and transformations shape remembered worlds. This selection foregrounds publicly shared Māori narratives and identifies Hawaiian figures without inventing a universal pantheon.",
"color":"#448FAD","symbol":"≈","motifs":["Ocean voyaging","Ancestral genealogies","World-shaping transformations"],"era":"Oral transmission continues alongside nineteenth-century and later written records; the timing and meanings of particular narratives vary by island, language, and community.","quote":"The horizon is a relationship, not the ending of the sea.",
"gods":[{"name":"Tāne","title":"Māori Ancestor of Forest Life","desc":"Tāne is a major Māori atua associated with forests and birds, prominently involved in separating earth and sky in widely shared but locally variable traditions."},{"name":"Tangaroa","title":"Māori Atua of the Sea","desc":"Tangaroa is associated with the sea and its creatures in Māori traditions; related names elsewhere in Polynesia carry different genealogies, responsibilities, and degrees of prominence."},{"name":"Pele","title":"Hawaiian Volcanic Deity","desc":"Pele is a Hawaiian deity of volcanic activity and landscape transformation, connected with specific places, family relationships, and living cultural practices rather than generic fire symbolism."}],
"stories":[{"id":"polynesia-1","title":"Māui Slows the Sun","tag":"Ingenuity & Time","summary":"In a widely shared Māori account, daylight passes too quickly for people's work. Māui and his brothers prepare ropes and confront the sun, persuading it to travel more slowly and give communities time to live well.","paragraphs":["Māui notices that the sun crosses the sky too quickly. The days are too short for people to finish the tasks that sustain them, and ordinary effort cannot change the pace of the heavens. He proposes a bold response to his brothers. Rather than simply complain about a powerful being, they will prepare together and confront the source of their difficulty.","They make strong ropes and journey toward the place where the sun rises. Hidden and ready, they cast their snares as its fierce light appears. In familiar Māori tellings, Māui also wields the jawbone of his ancestor. The struggle is dangerous, but preparation and coordinated action keep the sun from escaping immediately. A cosmic problem becomes approachable through shared labor and ancestral assistance.","Under pressure, the sun agrees to move more slowly, and people gain longer days for their activities. Other Polynesian traditions tell related Māui episodes with different settings, tools, and relationships. Those versions deserve their own contexts rather than being combined into one supposedly original story. Here the Māori narrative emphasizes audacity guided by practical need: a trickster's victory changes the conditions of communal life."]},{"id":"polynesia-2","title":"Tāne Opens the World to Light","tag":"Origins & Separation","summary":"Between Ranginui and Papatūānuku, their children live in darkness. Tāne pushes sky and earth apart, creating space for life, yet the parents' continuing sorrow makes the world's opening both an achievement and a lasting family separation.","paragraphs":["In widely shared Māori traditions, Ranginui the sky father and Papatūānuku the earth mother remain closely embraced. Their children live confined between them, with little room or light. The children debate how to change their condition. Some contemplate more violent action, while others seek separation. The issue is not simply the manufacture of space but the consequences of altering a foundational family relationship.","Several attempts fail before Tāne succeeds by placing his shoulders against earth and pushing upward with his legs. Sky and earth are forced apart, and light enters the world. Space becomes available for forests, birds, and human life. Yet not all the siblings consent. Tāwhirimātea, associated with winds and weather, opposes the separation and directs his anger against the others in ensuing conflict.","The parted parents continue to grieve, with rain and mist explained through their sorrow in familiar retellings. Creation therefore contains loss as well as possibility. These genealogical accounts remain connected with Māori identity, language, and relationships to place; they are not merely detached fantasy. Iwi and hapū preserve differing versions, so a short overview should never claim to supersede community knowledge or local authority."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Genealogical Relationships","desc":"In many Polynesian traditions, genealogy links people with land and divine ancestors, but names, sequences, and meanings are specific to communities rather than universally interchangeable."},{"name":"Te Pō and Te Ao Mārama","desc":"Māori expressions for darkness and the world of light occur within rich cosmological traditions whose sequences vary, resisting reduction to one standardized creation chart today."},{"name":"The Ocean as Connection","desc":"Ocean passages connect islands, ancestors, and living communities through skilled navigation and remembered voyages, challenging outsider portrayals of Polynesian places as isolated fragments without history."}],
"note":"Polynesia is not one religion. Māori atua, Hawaiian deities, and other island traditions require local names and contexts; living Indigenous knowledge includes responsibilities and boundaries that introductory retellings must respect."
},
{
"id":"aboriginal","name":"Aboriginal Australian traditions","en":"ABORIGINAL AUSTRALIA","region":"oceania","regionLabel":"Oceania","subtitle":"Many Countries, distinct peoples, and living ancestral relationships","description":"Aboriginal Australia includes hundreds of distinct peoples and languages, not one unified mythology. Ancestral narratives connect Country, kinship, law, and responsibility. This introduction uses only broadly public accounts, identifies particular communities where possible, and does not reproduce restricted knowledge. English labels such as Dreaming cannot fully convey the meanings of the many local concepts they are used to translate.","color":"#B17C65","symbol":"◎","motifs":["Country","Ancestral relationships","Living knowledge"],"era":"Living oral and ceremonial traditions, with diverse histories of public documentation","quote":"Country is not an empty stage; it holds relationships and obligations.",
"gods":[{"name":"Bunjil","title":"Ancestral Creator in Kulin Traditions","desc":"Bunjil is associated with the wedge-tailed eagle and with creation and law in publicly shared Kulin traditions of southeastern Australia. Accounts and custodianship remain locally specific."},{"name":"Baiame","title":"Ancestral Figure in Several Southeastern Traditions","desc":"Public descriptions connect Baiame with creation and law among particular southeastern peoples. These accounts must not be treated as a single pan-Aboriginal theology or universal identity."},{"name":"Rainbow Serpent traditions","title":"A Diverse Family of Publicly Discussed Motifs","desc":"Serpent beings associated with water and landscape occur in multiple traditions under distinct names. They are not one interchangeable deity, and much knowledge is community-specific or restricted."}],
"stories":[{"id":"aboriginal-1","title":"Bunjil and a Peopled Country","tag":"Public Accounts & Country","summary":"Public Kulin accounts describe Bunjil shaping a world of people, animals, and social relationships. This overview introduces a living ancestral figure without claiming to reveal ceremonial knowledge or replace community authority.","paragraphs":["In publicly shared accounts from southeastern Australia, Bunjil is an important ancestral creator associated with the wedge-tailed eagle. Creation is not merely the making of objects in empty space. Accounts link people and other beings with places, relationships, and responsibilities. The Kulin nations are distinct communities, and a general description cannot substitute for the authority of their knowledge holders.","Different public tellings describe Bunjil shaping land and living beings and establishing conditions for social life. Particular details belong to particular accounts; this atlas deliberately avoids combining them into a definitive script. The important introductory point is that law, kinship, and Country are connected. An ancestral narrative can concern how people should live, not only what happened at a remote beginning.","Bunjil remains present in contemporary cultural expression and public education. A visitor should learn from community-led resources and respect limits on access to stories, places, images, or names. Public availability does not erase custodianship. This short account therefore stops at general, openly shared information rather than retelling restricted episodes or presenting its own invented version as an authentic tradition."]},{"id":"aboriginal-2","title":"Waterways and Serpent Beings","tag":"Landscape & Responsibility","summary":"Across distinct Australian traditions, publicly discussed serpent beings may be associated with water and the shaping of Country. Their local names, narratives, and cultural permissions differ; there is no single universal Rainbow Serpent story.","paragraphs":["The English expression Rainbow Serpent gathers together beings and accounts from different Aboriginal traditions. That convenient label can obscure major differences. A serpent associated with a particular waterhole, river, or community cannot automatically be identified with one from another Country. Only the communities concerned can determine how their knowledge is properly named, shared, and understood.","Some public accounts describe ancestral movement as inseparable from the formation of waterways and other features of the land. Such relationships concern water, life, danger, and obligation. They are not simply decorative explanations for scenery. This atlas offers no composite adventure and no purported ceremonial teaching; those would misrepresent the diversity and authority of the traditions being introduced.","For further reading, begin with community-run cultural centers and public resources that clearly identify their custodians. Respect notices about images, names of deceased people, restricted locations, and material intended for particular audiences. The purpose of this page is to make the existence and specificity of these living relationships visible, not to turn them into freely interchangeable fantasy characters."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Country","desc":"Country includes land, waters, skies, beings, and relationships, with meanings shaped by distinct peoples. It cannot be reduced to property or scenic background."},{"name":"Ancestral Presence","desc":"English terms such as Dreaming translate diverse local concepts imperfectly. Ancestral relationships may concern continuing responsibilities rather than a single vanished prehistoric era."},{"name":"Knowledge and Custodianship","desc":"Access to knowledge follows community authority and cultural protocols. A public introduction must respect the boundaries around ceremonies, places, images, and particular narratives."}],"note":"There is no single Aboriginal Australian religion. This page provides only public, introductory information and never claims to disclose restricted knowledge or speak for any community."
},
{
"id":"korea","name":"Korean mythic traditions","en":"KOREA","region":"asia","regionLabel":"Asia","subtitle":"Sacred descent, founding ancestors, and journeys beyond the living world","description":"Korean mythic traditions include founding narratives preserved in historical collections, local legends, and shamanic ritual narratives that continue to be performed. They are not one fixed pantheon. Dangun’s ancestry, Jumong’s founding journey, and Princess Bari’s passage toward the dead offer different ways of connecting sacred authority, kinship, suffering, and care.","color":"#A6B2A5","symbol":"☷","motifs":["Founding ancestors","Sacred mountains","Ritual journeys"],"era":"Medieval collections preserve earlier traditions; oral and ritual narratives continue today","quote":"A path across the mountains may also become a path toward belonging.",
"gods":[{"name":"Hwanung","title":"Heavenly Descendant in the Dangun Account","desc":"In the Dangun narrative preserved in the Samguk yusa, Hwanung descends to the human world with followers and sacred insignia, governing aspects of life beneath a sacred tree."},{"name":"Dangun","title":"Founding Ancestor of Gojoseon","desc":"Dangun is presented as Gojoseon’s founder in a foundational narrative connecting heavenly descent, a bear’s transformation, and kingship. Narrative chronology is not independent archaeological dating."},{"name":"Princess Bari","title":"Ritual Heroine of Care and the Dead","desc":"Bari is abandoned at birth yet undertakes a perilous journey to obtain life-restoring water or medicine. Her role and narrative vary across regional shamanic traditions."}],
"stories":[{"id":"korea-1","title":"The Bear Who Became Human","tag":"Origins & Transformation","summary":"In the Dangun account, a bear and a tiger seek human life. The bear endures a trial and becomes a woman; her child with Hwanung is Dangun, a founding ancestor of Gojoseon.","paragraphs":["The Samguk yusa, compiled in the thirteenth century, preserves an account of Hwanung descending from heaven to the human world. His followers and sacred insignia accompany him, and a sacred tree marks a place of governance. This is a medieval record of a founding tradition, not a contemporary eyewitness chronicle of the ancient events it narrates.","A bear and a tiger ask to become human. They receive a discipline involving seclusion and particular foods, commonly described in translation as mugwort and garlic. The tiger abandons the trial, while the bear persists and becomes a woman. The contrast makes transformation a matter of endurance as well as desire, although interpretations of the animal figures differ.","The woman, often called Ungnyeo, later bears Dangun through her union with Hwanung. Dangun founds Gojoseon, joining heavenly ancestry with the beginnings of a polity. The account has acquired varied historical and national meanings over time. Its traditional dates should not be confused with archaeological proof, and its continued importance cannot be reduced to a single modern interpretation."]},{"id":"korea-2","title":"Princess Bari and the Water of Life","tag":"Ritual Journey & Care","summary":"Abandoned because she is a daughter, Bari later journeys to obtain medicine or life-restoring water for her parents. Her story connects rejection, endurance, compassion, and guidance for the dead in Korean ritual traditions.","paragraphs":["Bari is born after other daughters and rejected by parents who had hoped for a son. Rescued or raised away from the palace in different versions, she survives beyond the family that abandoned her. When illness later threatens her parents, the child considered unwanted becomes the one willing to undertake a difficult journey on their behalf.","Her route passes through perilous or otherworldly regions in search of life-restoring water, flowers, or medicine. Some tellings describe long service, demanding tasks, or a relationship with a guardian at the destination. Details vary by region and ritual lineage. A short retelling should preserve that variation rather than turn several different performances into one supposedly authoritative text.","Bari returns with the means of restoration and becomes associated with guiding the dead or aiding their passage. Her significance is not confined to exemplary obedience: readers and practitioners also find suffering, agency, and care within the journey. The narrative belongs to living Korean shamanic traditions, and its meaning arises in performance and community as well as on the page."]}],
"cosmos":[{"name":"Heaven and Descent","desc":"Founding narratives link political or ancestral beginnings with descent from heaven. The identities and genealogies vary; they do not constitute one universal Korean creation account."},{"name":"Sacred Mountains and Trees","desc":"Mountains and trees can mark relationships between communities, territory, and the sacred. Specific locations and meanings differ across texts, regions, and practices."},{"name":"Journeys of the Dead","desc":"Ritual narratives such as Bari’s address passage, care, and relations between living and dead. They remain connected with regional performance traditions, not only literary collections."}],"note":"Korean founding traditions and shamanic narratives are distinct genres. Regional practitioners preserve different versions; this introduction does not claim one fixed pantheon or speak for all Korean beliefs."
}
];
